  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Tata Nexon in India?",
      "a": "A well-maintained Nexon retains approximately 71% of its ex-showroom price after two years, 62% after three years, and 47% after five years. For a 2022 XZ+ petrol with 25,000 to 50,000 km, the current fair-market band is roughly ₹7.11 to ₹8.18 lakh."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the Nexon hold value as well as the Brezza?",
      "a": "Close, but not quite. The Nexon retains about 62% at three years versus roughly 64% for the Maruti Brezza, a narrow gap that reflects Maruti's larger service network and higher buyer familiarity in tier-2 cities. In metros with strong SUV demand like Bengaluru and Mumbai, the two trade within a point of each other."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the pre-2023 Nexon worth buying used?",
      "a": "Yes, if the car has been serviced regularly and has less than 60,000 km. The pre-facelift Nexon has a solid safety rating, mature drivetrain, and now trades 10 to 15% below the equivalent post-facelift car. The main risks to inspect are the AMT actuator on 2019 to 2021 units and clear-coat peeling on Foliage Green and Daytona Grey paint."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale for a Nexon?",
      "a": "Bengaluru and Mumbai currently trade at approximately +3 to +4% above the national mid, driven by strong SUV demand. Kolkata is soft at −2%."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Nexon depreciate per year?",
      "a": "Roughly 18% in year one, 11% additional in year two, then 8 to 9% per year through year five. The five-year retention of about 47% is 1 percentage point below the sub-4m SUV segment average of 48%."
    }
  ],
